Here's the test:

```go
func TestTokenExpiry(t *testing.T) {
    tok := NewToken("abc", 100*time.Millisecond)
    time.Sleep(50 * time.Millisecond)
    expected := false
    assert.Equal(t, expected, tok.IsExpired())
}
```

And IsExpired:

```go
func (t *Token) IsExpired() bool {
    return time.Now().After(t.createdAt.Add(t.ttl))
}
```

```go
func NewToken(val string, ttl time.Duration) *Token {
    return &Token{value: val, ttl: ttl, createdAt: time.Now()}
}
```

Bumping the margin papers over it but doesn't fix the race — just makes it rarer. The real fix is to inject a clock so the test owns both reads:

```go
type Clock interface { Now() time.Time }

type Token struct {
    value     string
    ttl       time.Duration
    createdAt time.Time
    clock     Clock
}

func (t *Token) IsExpired() bool {
    return t.clock.Now().After(t.createdAt.Add(t.ttl))
}
```

Then in the test use a fake clock you advance manually. No wall-clock reads, no race.

